00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.720
Yet another text to image generator.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:49.810
Who would have thought that Google Brain would
also take a shot at diffusion models generating

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.560
images from text?

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:57.831
Recently, we have seen quite a lot of advancements
in the text-to-image generation realm, so

00:01:57.831 --> 00:02:01.970
here is a short recap of the most important
corner stones.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:08.080
The first bang was caused by DALL-E by OpenAI,
an autoregressive model that could take in

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.470
text and generate impressive images, even
though a bit blurry.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:19.080
DALL-E is a GPT-like model, which given a
piece of text and the start of an image, generates

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.550
the image pixel by pixel, row by row.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:28.010
Then the second, bigger bang was made again
by OpenAI, but this time with GLIDE, which

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:32.670
is a diffusion model, so a very different
kind of beast compared to DALL-E.

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:36.980
If you do not know what diffusion models are,
check out our previous video where we explain

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:40.880
diffusion models in general and GLIDE in particular.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.370
In the meantime, we have seen approaches like
VQGAN-CLIP, which uses multimodal text embeddings

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:50.190
by CLIP to generate images from them with
a VQGAN.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.319
I remember these images flooding my Twitter
feed for a long time.

00:02:54.319 --> 00:02:58.880
Then we’ve been delighted by diffusion models
similar to GLIDE, but unlike GLIDE that was

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:06.160
generating relatively small images of 256
by 256 after up-sampling, this method successfully

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:10.360
applies diffusion models to generate 1024
x 1024-pixel images.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:16.590
But, you know, not coming from a large company,
so without much traction on social media,

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:20.030
this work was not noticed by a wide audience.

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:24.520
Speaking of social media traction, this is
what DALL-E 2 got in full.

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:30.380
After all, it was presented by OpenAI with
a big bang, as being the follower model of

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:31.380
DALL-E.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:34.120
But if you ask me, this was a very successful
PR move.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:39.040
DALL-E 2, being a diffusion model and not
an autoregressive model like DALL-E, felt

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.430
like a successful increment on GLIDE rather
than DALL-E.

00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:48.540
DALL-E 2 is very much like GLIDE with minimal
conceptual changes: instead of generating

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:54.569
the image from noise, it generates the image
from the CLIP image encodings corresponding

00:03:54.569 --> 00:03:57.870
to the CLIP encoding of the textual prompt.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:03.020
It’s interesting to see how the huge jump
from DALL-E to DALL-E 2 only feels large if

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.170
one doesn’t see the advancements in between,
like GLIDE.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:12.160
Do you think this would have made the same
splash if they named GLIDE DALLE-2 and DALL-E

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.160
2 DALL-E 3?

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:15.530
We don’t think so.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:20.829
We do not want to minimize the work done here
or the achievements, we just want to emphasize

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:26.770
how the naming of things and the right introduction
to the public, make them more attractive than

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.999
they would otherwise be.

00:04:28.999 --> 00:04:34.919
Now we get to the latest and greatest as of
today: Imagen also a diffusion model, like

00:04:34.919 --> 00:04:36.870
GLIDE or DALL-E 2.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:41.259
Coming from Google Brain, it made and still
makes splashes on social media.

00:04:41.259 --> 00:04:43.219
So how does it work?

00:04:43.219 --> 00:04:47.379
We won’t cover diffusion models in detail
again, since we already did in a previous

00:04:47.379 --> 00:04:50.740
video, do check it out and come back again
afterwards.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.629
In a nutshell, a diffusion model works like
this: to prepare training data, we do the

00:04:55.629 --> 00:05:01.069
forward diffusion process where we take an
image and add more and more noise to it, until

00:05:01.069 --> 00:05:04.080
it looks like just noise.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.460
Then we use one single model, like a U-Net,
to reverse each of these steps, which is the

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:11.220
backward diffusion process.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:18.509
Imagen is a diffusion model too and is surprisingly
similar to GLIDE, rather than to DALL-E 2.

00:05:18.509 --> 00:05:23.749
In other words, if you already know about
GLIDE, you will not have a hard time understanding

00:05:23.749 --> 00:05:24.749
Imagen.

00:05:24.749 --> 00:05:29.419
Both GLIDE and Imagen use a text transformer
to encode the language prompt.

00:05:29.419 --> 00:05:35.610
While GLIDE trains a transformer from scratch
for this on the training data of DALL-E 2

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.599
consisting of images and captions,

00:05:37.599 --> 00:05:45.169
Imagen takes an off-the shelf, huge language
model, namely T5-XXL which has seen lots and

00:05:45.169 --> 00:05:49.539
lots of text, so text with more variation
than just image captions.

00:05:49.539 --> 00:05:54.969
And the language model is frozen, which seems
crucial for keeping its text processing capabilities

00:05:54.969 --> 00:05:59.849
instead of overly specializing it on the captions
of images.

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:04.659
At training time, the text-to-image diffusion
model learns to generate an image conditioned

00:06:04.659 --> 00:06:06.919
on the text embedding.

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:12.710
Unlike GLIDE, imagen uses a smaller version
of the U-Net to learn the backward diffusion

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:13.789
steps.

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:18.629
A concatenated super-resolution diffusion
model is trained to upscale the generated

00:06:18.629 --> 00:06:26.080
low-resolution image of 64 x 64 pixels, to
produce a higher resolution image of 256 x

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:32.789
256 which can be then further upscaled to
1024 x 1024 by a super-resolution diffusion

00:06:32.789 --> 00:06:38.940
model, which is a nice addition to just diffusion
models that generate images from text.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:44.969
Like GLIDE, Imagen uses classifier-free guidance
at test time to further enhance the impact

00:06:44.969 --> 00:06:46.159
of the text.

00:06:46.159 --> 00:06:52.979
So, when generating a sample, one runs the
whole diffusion process once with text conditioning,

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:56.889
and another whole process without any text
conditioning.

00:06:56.889 --> 00:07:02.130
One computes the difference between the diffusion
step with text and without text.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:07.689
With this difference, we now know in which
direction to move if we want to go from no-text

00:07:07.689 --> 00:07:08.689
to text.

00:07:08.689 --> 00:07:14.369
So, we take the text-less generation and add
this difference scaled by quite a lot such

00:07:14.369 --> 00:07:19.279
that the output of the model without text
information is heavily extrapolated into the

00:07:19.279 --> 00:07:20.939
direction of text information.

00:07:20.939 --> 00:07:27.409
And yes, this is a weird hack such that the
image generation is more faithful to the text,

00:07:27.409 --> 00:07:32.399
but it seems to work both for Imagen and for
GLIDE, so it does the job.

00:07:32.399 --> 00:07:37.249
For classifier-free guidance in Imagen, the
authors also introduce a dynamic thresholding

00:07:37.249 --> 00:07:42.139
method to make sure that by extrapolating
into the direction of the text, we do not

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:44.949
generate pixels that are out of the allowed
range.

00:07:44.949 --> 00:07:49.909
This reportedly results in better photorealism
and better image-text alignment, showing that

00:07:49.909 --> 00:07:55.099
the weird hack of classifier-free guidance
has spawned small hacks on its own.

00:07:55.099 --> 00:07:59.599
Since Imagen draws so much attention, is there
something that it can deliver, that DALL-E

00:07:59.599 --> 00:08:01.039
2 does not?

00:08:01.039 --> 00:08:06.999
Imagen advertises itself with, we cite, “an
unprecedented degree of photorealism and a

00:08:06.999 --> 00:08:09.630
deep level of language understanding”.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:16.129
Well, in terms of photorealism, the authors
of Imagen show side by side comparisons to

00:08:16.129 --> 00:08:18.800
DALL-E 2 and claim higher photorealism.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.809
I do not know, really, if you ask me, I like
the pictures of Imagen more, but Ms. Coffee

00:08:24.809 --> 00:08:26.360
Bean is more a fan of DALL-E 2.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:32.349
The state-of-the-art is so good now, that
evaluating the models is subjective already.

00:08:32.349 --> 00:08:38.539
At least, the Imagen paper proposes a direction
on how to evaluate these text-to-image generators

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:43.289
more consistently: The authors introduce DrawBench,
a suite of,

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:49.260
(ok, it’s basically a Google Sheet), so
a suite of 200 text prompts probing for certain

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:55.089
model capacities, like, we cite form the paper:
“compositionality, cardinality, spatial

00:08:55.089 --> 00:09:00.630
relations, the ability to handle complex text
prompts or prompts with rare words, and they

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:06.279
include creative prompts that push the limits
of models’ ability to generate highly implausible

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:10.300
scenes well beyond the scope of the training
data.”

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:11.449
End quote.

00:09:11.449 --> 00:09:16.639
On this benchmark prompts, the authors let
different models generate images and let human

00:09:16.639 --> 00:09:19.089
annotators rate the generations.

00:09:19.089 --> 00:09:23.300
In general, Imagen performs better on DrawBench
than competitors.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:28.399
But I would really like to see a larger benchmark
where others can contribute to, such that

00:09:28.399 --> 00:09:33.670
testing cannot fall victim to wishful thinking,
maybe unconsciously introduced by benchmark

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:37.839
makers when they want to prove their model
to be the best.

00:09:37.839 --> 00:09:42.680
Maybe wishful thinking is not present here,
but I hope everybody sees why more contributors

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:44.709
to these benchmarks would be beneficial.

00:09:44.709 --> 00:09:51.029
Let’s look at even more examples of Imagen’s
capabilities show in the paper: It looks like

00:09:51.029 --> 00:09:54.509
Imagen is generally better with generating
text than GLIDE.

00:09:54.509 --> 00:09:59.240
When it comes to faithfulness to the text
prompt and language understanding, Imagen

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:03.139
delivers great results (at least from the
examples we can see).

00:10:03.139 --> 00:10:08.259
In this example, Imagen can draw a panda making
latte art, while GLIDE gets it wrong and draws

00:10:08.259 --> 00:10:11.620
a human making latte art containing a panda.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:16.129
At least for the language understanding part,
it looks like using a full-fledged frozen

00:10:16.129 --> 00:10:21.160
language model like T5, trained on diverse
text, is crucial in capturing language meaning

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:26.089
and diversity, that CLIP’s language encoder
cannot because it was trained on image captions

00:10:26.089 --> 00:10:29.240
representing only a small subset of natural
language.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:34.120
The already famous example of a horse riding
an astronaut is something that both Imagen

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:35.850
and GLIDE get wrong.

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:37.900
Or so we thought because look!

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:43.459
Another prompt, another picture because when
Neyshabur prompted Imagen with “a horse

00:10:43.459 --> 00:10:48.920
riding on shoulders of an astronaut”, he
helped Imagen generate something better.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:53.839
But in general, compositionality is something
these models still struggle with and I am

00:10:53.839 --> 00:10:59.029
not sure we can use the word “understanding”
with models that can draw a horse riding on

00:10:59.029 --> 00:11:03.529
shoulders of an astronaut, but not just “a
horse riding an astronaut”.

00:11:03.529 --> 00:11:09.300
I mean, we have incredible large models that
can do so many things, yet prompt engineering

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:14.779
feels like school times when the teacher has
to pose the questions just right such that

00:11:14.779 --> 00:11:19.680
the student remembers the exact formulation
from the book and only then deliver the correct

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:20.680
answer.

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:25.750
But do not get us wrong here, what we see
are huge advancements, not imaginable only

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:27.120
a year ago.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:31.569
If you want to read more detailed criticism
of compositionality, check out Gary Marcus’

00:11:31.569 --> 00:11:34.040
post on this, linked in the description below.

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:38.310
The bad news about Imagen, like with DALL-E
2 is that we have to believe everything the

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:43.820
authors or people with access to these models
show us after they picked the good cherries.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:48.480
We cannot try these models out unless someone
decides to generate an image from our prompt

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.560
when writing on the right thread on Twitter,
find the link in the video description if

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.560
interested.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:56.570
At least for DALL-E 2 there is a waitlist.

00:11:56.570 --> 00:12:01.070
Look, we’ve met this guy last week at the
ACL conference and he was kind enough to let

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:03.810
DALL-E 2 freestyle some coffee beans!

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:09.269
I really need such a model to increase the
quality of the visualisations in our videos!

00:12:09.269 --> 00:12:14.380
But now back to Imagen: it’s not surprising
they do not release the model: They possibly

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:16.389
want to make money with it.

00:12:16.389 --> 00:12:22.620
Even more, it was trained on internal datasets
of around 460M image-text pairs, on top of

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:28.580
the publicly available Laion dataset, with
around 400M image-text pairs.

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:33.910
So that you get an idea about the size, CLIP
was also trained on 400 million image-text

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:34.910
pairs.

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:39.839
And when someone trains on internal datasets,
they usually do not release the models, because

00:12:39.839 --> 00:12:44.639
we know that models are not good at keeping
secrecy about their training data.

00:12:44.639 --> 00:12:50.370
It is also interesting to see that none of
the examples we see from Imagen contain human

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:54.950
faces, but just humanoid shapes like astronauts
or robots.

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:59.199
I wonder why this is, since we have seen faces
with DALL-E 2.

00:12:59.199 --> 00:13:06.189
I cannot imagine Imagen (pun intended) not
being able to draw human faces, maybe it is

00:13:06.189 --> 00:13:09.800
just Google Brain trying to avoid discussions
on biases?

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:12.629
What are your takes about these models?

00:13:12.629 --> 00:13:18.709
Honestly, count me impressed, but Ms. Coffee
Bean is not entirely blown away by Imagen.

00:13:18.709 --> 00:13:24.389
She is happy to see the initiative on DrawBench
that starts (but does not complete!) a direction

00:13:24.389 --> 00:13:27.610
towards comparing these generative models.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:32.029
It’s interesting to see how this benchmark
evolves and how other benchmarks might come

00:13:32.029 --> 00:13:37.470
into being, because we think that evaluating
these models is maybe more than just “can

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:42.279
the model draw this or that?”, so the fatefulness
to the text prompt,

00:13:42.279 --> 00:13:48.110
but that these models might get ratings on
more aspects, like “originality” where

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:53.260
the model goes a bit crazy and does not stick
exactly to the prompt – especially useful

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:58.750
for more creative situations for generating
something surprising but pleasant.

00:13:58.750 --> 00:14:03.500
Maybe photorealism should also be a slider,
because it is maybe sometimes also a more

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:08.029
cartoonish, DALL-E style thing that people
would like more.

00:14:08.029 --> 00:14:13.100
In general, we might see a lot more work in
controllability of these diffusion models,

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:15.480
to get exactly what we want from such image
generating models.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:20.670
The good thing about text-to-image models
is that the slider is basically implemented

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:23.399
with natural language!

00:14:23.399 --> 00:14:29.629
Only that unlike language-based diffusion
models, sliders do not need any prompting!

00:14:29.629 --> 00:14:34.890
“Let’s think step by step” and “let’s
hope for the best” for the future of image

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:36.740
generation!

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:38.620
See you next time!

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:40.290
Okay, bye!

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:44.670
If you are interested in these image generation
models like DALL-E, maybe you want to check

00:14:44.670 --> 00:14:49.970
out this Weights&amp;Biases Report on DALL-E mini,
an open-source replica of DALL-E!

